(7774110, 1291843, 4008, '<td valign="top">
<table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LevT:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by willem:</b></div><hr/><i> Beautiful image. Great mood. I would love to learn which duotone colors and curves you have used to achieve this. I tried but am unable to. </i></td></tr></table> Thank you! I used Silver Efex Pro Photoshop plug-in to convert to B&amp;W and add strong toning. It has separate color and strength controls for darker (silver) and lighter (paper) tones. </i></td></tr></table>
<br/>Thank you, I will try that. Not sure but it seems different in separating the two tones from regular photoshop duotone, probably because the traditional (photoshop) toning is simulating a printing process with two tones on white paper?</td>', 'Originally posted by LevT: Originally posted by willem: Beautiful image. Great mood. I would love to learn which duotone colors and curves you have used to achieve this. I tried but am unable to.  Thank you! I used Silver Efex Pro Photoshop plug-in to convert to B&W and add strong toning. It has separate color and strength controls for darker (silver) and lighter (paper) tones. 
Thank you, I will try that. Not sure but it seems different in separating the two tones from regular photoshop duotone, probably because the traditional (photoshop) toning is simulating a printing process with two tones on white paper?', '2024-12-26 04:46:03.000000', NULL, 0),
